Everything seems fine. One more thing I want to reconsider is the ordering of cleanup inside `finalize_client`:

```c
static inline void finalize_client(CLIENT* client) {
	g_thread_pool_free(tpool, FALSE, TRUE);
	cleanup_cow(client);
	do_run(client->server->postrun, client->exportname);
	...
}
```

g_thread_pool_free waits for pending requests → then cleanup_cow → safe. ✓

Another consideration — `do_run(postrun)` runs after the diff file has been deleted. The postrun script might want to inspect the diff... but the semantics is "delete on disconnect," and postrun runs "after the client disconnects." Since the diff file is per-session temporary data, deletion before postrun is correct, and I think it's also consistent with the description that "writes are lost on disconnect."

Now, the last item: the man page already documents this behavior, so no changes needed there. The `-c` usage string already exists too.

Wait, one more thing to check — is the man page's mention of `sparse_cow` "nbd-server lseeks after each 4096-byte block" consistent with the implementation? Our non-sparse mode appends pages to the tail and does pwrite at the mapped position — the semantics match.

Also, there's a statement at line 314 of the man page: "splice cannot be used in combination with TLS or copyonwrite mode... if both splice and copyonwrite are enabled, the server exits with an appropriate error message" — this is implemented in parse_cfile (existing). ✓

